{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"bland-vixenish-longspur","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Summary\n\n`rebar-container-writable-usage-high` went ALARM at 2026-09-17 18:01 PDT on the Gerrit host and is **still climbing**. Every bounding mechanism the design provides is inapplicable here, so nothing is currently arresting the growth.\n\n## Observed, from CloudWatch `rebar/host`\n\n| Hour (local) | `container_writable_bytes` | `container_exited_bytes` | `container_writable_used_percent` |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 13:00 | 1,745,000,000 | 0 | 81 |\n| 14:00 | 1,745,000,000 | 0 | 81 |\n| 15:00 | 1,745,000,000 | 0 | 81 |\n| 16:00 | 1,758,000,000 | 0 | 81 |\n| 17:00 | 1,848,000,000 | 0 | 86 |\n| 18:00 | 1,939,000,000 | 0 | 90 |\n\nFlat through the afternoon, then inflecting at 16:00 and climbing roughly 90 MB/hour since.\n\n## Why nothing is bounding it\n\nThe alarm's own description sets out the two mechanisms, and **both are ruled out by the metrics**:\n\n1. **The reaper can only remove EXITED containers.** `container_exited_bytes` reads **0** at every datapoint. There is no reclaimable debris. Forcing a pass with `container-cap.sh --reap` would free nothing, so the standard remediation is a no-op here. The bytes belong to running services.\n2. **An overlay2 per-container quota is the only thing that bounds a running container.** `container_quota_enforceable` reads **0**. This host cannot have one.\n\nThis is the condition bug `3a52-2d79-320d-4fed` already confirmed in a different incident: \"`prune_docker_caches` cannot reclaim a **running** container's writable layer at all.\" The design anticipated this state and exposes `container_quota_enforceable` precisely so it can be detected — what it does not do is bound the growth when the answer is 0.\n\n## Why this is P1\n\nThis is the same class as `3276-2f81-8c75-4ddd`, the 2026-09-02 root-volume exhaustion that took Gerrit **fully offline for about five hours**. Epic `6202-e1c7-c57f-4897` was built to give every root-disk consumer a ceiling, with S5 (`910b`) covering writable container layers. The alarm firing with both mechanisms inapplicable indicates that ceiling is not effective on this host.\n\nAt 90% of share and roughly 90 MB/hour, the remaining headroom is on the order of a couple of hours. Losing Gerrit stops all landing, and it also takes down the MCP gate workspace that plan-review and completion-verification depend on.\n\n## Investigation required first — do not remediate blind\n\nPer the alarm's stated diagnosis path, establish which container owns the bytes before changing anything:\n\n- `docker ps -a --size` to attribute the writable bytes to specific containers.\n- Determine whether the growth is one container or several, and whether the inflection at 16:00 correlates with a specific workload starting.\n- Determine what inside that container is writing — logs, caches, scratch, or accumulated artifacts — since the remedy differs completely between them.\n\nA live service growing its own layer is an **application problem**, not a cleanup one. Do not treat it as a cleanup problem because cleanup is the tool that happens to exist.\n\n**NEVER delete anything under `/var/lib/docker/overlay2` by hand.**\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The container or containers owning the writable bytes are named, with a byte figure each. Verified by `docker ps -a --size` output recorded on the ticket.\n- [ ] The specific paths inside the growing container that account for the growth are identified. Verified by a per-path size listing recorded on the ticket.\n- [ ] The cause of the 16:00 inflection is stated, or stated as not determinable with the evidence available. Verified by reading the finding against the metric series above.\n- [ ] A determination is recorded of whether the growth is bounded by anything at all once the container restarts. Verified by the stated finding.\n- [ ] `container_writable_used_percent` returns below the alarm threshold and stays there for at least 6 consecutive datapoints. Verified by the alarm returning to OK.\n- [ ] The remedy bounds the growth rather than reclaiming it once. Verified by stating which mechanism now bounds it, and, if none can, escalating rather than closing.\n\n## Escalation clause\n\nIf the growth cannot be bounded on this host because `container_quota_enforceable` is 0 and the workload legitimately needs the space, do **not** resolve this by raising the cap and closing. State the constraint plainly, name what would have to change to bound it, and escalate. An unbounded root-disk consumer with a raised ceiling is the precondition for `3276` recurring, not a fix for it.\n\n## Grounding\n\n- `infra/scripts/container-cap.sh` — the cap value and `--print-env`, plus `--reap`.\n- `infra/scripts/observability.sh` §2i — publishes `container_writable_used_percent`.\n- `infra/runbooks/review-bot-ops.md` — runbook named by the alarm.\n- Host: `i-00880b2c7f13527c5`.","id":"ce66-59ef-d6d7-4c7a","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"[root-disk-pressure] Writable container layers climbing unbounded: reaper scope is empty and overlay2 quota is unenforceable on this host"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1789695187911203045,"uuid":"189cdaa7-70cb-4ef8-b05a-87dfcb09216f"}